\section{Preliminaries}

We begin with a computational substrate defined by three primitive
operations:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Branching} $B$ : state divergence
    \item \textbf{Fusion} $F$ : state convergence
    \item \textbf{Bias} $\beta$ : directional weighting of transitions
\end{itemize}

Let the state space be


\[
S = \{ s_i \mid i \in \mathbb{N} \}.
\]



A transition is a triple


\[
T = (s_i, \; \beta, \; s_j)
\]


with $\beta \in \mathbb{R}$ representing directional influence.

\section{Core Dynamics}

Define the evolution operator


\[
\mathcal{E} : S \to \mathcal{P}(S)
\]


by


\[
\mathcal{E}(s) = B(s) \cup F(s) \cup \beta(s).
\]



The global system is the directed multigraph


\[
\mathcal{G} = (S, E)
\]


where


\[
E = \{ (s_i, s_j, \beta_{ij}) \mid s_j \in \mathcal{E}(s_i) \}.
\]



\section{Canonical Pattern}

Empirically, repeated application of $\mathcal{E}$ produces a stable
structural attractor characterized by:

\begin{itemize}
    \item radial branching,
    \item reconvergent fusion hubs,
    \item a dense central core,
    \item layered symmetry.
\end{itemize}

We call this the \emph{canonical archetype}.

Formally, define the limit object


\[
\mathcal{C} = \lim_{n \to \infty} \mathcal{E}^n(s_0)
\]


whenever the limit exists in the sense of graph convergence.

\section{Projection Hierarchy}

We distinguish several projections of $\mathcal{C}$:

\subsection{Geometric Projection}


\[
\pi_{\mathrm{geom}} : \mathcal{C} \to \mathbb{R}^2
\]


producing a spatial embedding.

\subsection{Causal Projection}


\[
\pi_{\mathrm{causal}} : \mathcal{C} \to \text{CausalSet}
\]


preserving influence relations.

\subsection{Computational Projection}


\[
\pi_{\mathrm{comp}} : \mathcal{C} \to \text{StateMachine}
\]


preserving transition structure.

\subsection{Information-Theoretic Projection}


\[
\pi_{\mathrm{info}} : \mathcal{C} \to \mathbb{R}^+
\]


mapping nodes to entropy or information content.

\section{Universality Claim}

We say the substrate is \emph{representationally universal} if for any
structured system


\[
X \in \mathcal{D}
\]


(where $\mathcal{D}$ is the class of networks, dynamical systems,
computational processes, etc.), there exists an encoding


\[
\Phi : X \to \mathcal{G}
\]


such that the dynamics of $X$ are preserved under $\mathcal{E}$.

This is weaker than computational universality but stronger than domain-specific modeling.

\section{Reality Engine Criterion}

A system qualifies as a \emph{reality engine} if:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item it admits a universal encoding $\Phi$ for diverse domains,
    \item it supports internal simulation via $\mathcal{E}$,
    \item it supports decoding back into domain-specific observables.
\end{enumerate}

If $\mathcal{G}$ satisfies these, then


\[
\mathcal{G}
\]


is a candidate reality engine.

\section{Interpretation}

The canonical object $\mathcal{C}$ is not a model of physical spacetime,
but a \emph{computational shadow} of any system representable in the
branch--fuse substrate.

It captures:
\begin{itemize}
    \item attractors,
    \item symmetries,
    \item bottlenecks,
    \item causal horizons,
    \item stability regions.
\end{itemize}

